### Short answer  
**It’s not a bug.**  
When a style **doesn’t contain a `glyphs` field**, MapLibre GL JS (v5.11 +) automatically falls back to its **local-font rendering path**.  
Each name that appears in a layer’s `text-font` array is treated exactly like a CSS `font-family` list:

1. **If a matching web-font is loaded in the page (via `@font-face` or a Google-Fonts link) or a system font with that name exists,** the text will be drawn with that font.  
2. **If the browser can’t find a font with that exact name,** it uses its normal CSS fallback chain (usually a generic “sans-serif” or the OS default). That’s why you see a “generic” font instead of the one you wrote in the style.

So the behavior you’re seeing is **expected** – the style is telling MapLibre “don’t ask a glyph server, just use whatever font the browser can find for these names.”  

Below is a deeper dive into **what’s happening**, why you get a generic font, and how to make the intended font appear (or, if you prefer, switch back to a glyph-server approach).

---

## 1. What MapLibre does when `glyphs` is omitted

| Step | What MapLibre GL JS does | Why it matters |
|------|--------------------------|----------------|
| **a.** Detects that the style has no `glyphs` property. | Enters *local-fonts mode* (introduced in PR #4564, shipped in v5.11.0). | No network request for `…/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf` is made. |
| **b.** Reads each layer’s `text-font` array. | Treats the array as a **cascading list of font-family names**, exactly like CSS. | The first name that the browser can resolve wins. |
| **c.** For each glyph needed, asks the browser’s text engine to rasterise it (TinySDF for ideographs, native text shaping for complex scripts). | No SDF glyph PBFs are used. | Works offline and avoids many HTTP requests, but you must supply the font yourself. |
| **d.** If the name cannot be resolved, the browser falls back to its default generic family (`sans-serif`, `serif`, etc.). | You see a generic font that varies by OS / browser. | This is the “wrong” font you’re observing. |

> **Key point:** The fallback is **only in GL JS**. MapLibre Native still requires a `glyphs` URL (or a `font-faces` entry that points at a local file) – otherwise the layer will be invisible or will fall back to the default glyph server you configured.

---

## 2. Why you’re getting a “generic” font

1. **No web-font is actually loaded** – you only wrote the name in `text-font`, but the browser never received a `@font-face` rule or a `<link>` to a font file that carries that exact name.  
2. **The name you used does not match the font’s internal family name** – e.g. you wrote `"MyBrand"` but the TTF’s `name` table says `"My Brand Regular"`. CSS (and therefore MapLibre) does a case-insensitive match on the *family* name, not on the filename.  
3. **The font is loaded, but the name you used is not in the `font-faces` mapping** – if you rely on the style’s `font-faces` property (instead of a plain CSS `@font-face`), the name must appear as a key there.  
4. **CORS / loading error** – the font file is blocked or 404s, so the browser discards it and falls back.

All of the above result in the browser’s normal CSS fallback chain being used, which looks “generic”.

---

## 3. How to make the intended font appear

### Option A – Load the font as a normal web font (the simplest)

```html
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
```

or

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "MyBrand";
  src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: 400;
  font-style: normal;
}
```

Then in your style:

```json
{
  "type": "symbol",
  "layout": {
    "text-field": ["get", "name"],
    "text-font": ["MyBrand", "sans-serif"]
  }
}
```

*Result:* The browser finds “MyBrand”, rasterises the glyphs locally, and MapLibre draws exactly that font.

### Option B – Declare the font via the style’s `font-faces` property (useful when you want the same definition for GL JS **and** Native)

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-FFFF"]   // optional, can limit to Latin etc.
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["MyBrand", "sans-serif"]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*What happens:*  
- GL JS loads the TTF/OTF via XHR (subject to CORS).  
- Native reads the same `font-faces` entry and loads the file from the device’s storage (the URL must be reachable from the native app, e.g. a `file://` URL or a bundled asset).  
- Both SDKs use the font for **all** scripts that the browser can shape (Latin, Arabic, Devanagari, etc.).  

> **Tip:** If you only need the font for Latin characters, you can restrict the `unicode-range` to `U+0000-00FF`. Anything outside that range will fall back to the default glyph server (if you have one) or to the generic system font.

### Option C – Keep the `glyphs` URL and let the server serve SDF PBFs (the classic approach)

If you prefer the original MapLibre workflow (use SDF glyphs, guarantee identical appearance across browsers and devices, and avoid loading large TTFs on the client), add a `glyphs` field:

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://myselfhosted.example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [...]
}
```

*When to choose this:*  
- You have a **static set of fonts** that you want to guarantee looks the same everywhere (including Native).  
- You are already running a glyph-server (e.g., the one that ships with OpenMapTiles).  
- You want to avoid the extra download of full TTF/OTF files on low-bandwidth clients.

> **Note:** If you later omit `glyphs` again, you’ll revert to the local-font fallback described above.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting checklist

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Text appears, but in a different font than `text-font` | No web-font loaded, or name mismatch | Verify the font is loaded (check Network tab) and that the `font-family` name matches exactly the string in `text-font`. |
| Console shows `Unable to load glyph range … Rendering codepoint U+... locally instead.` | You *did* set a `glyphs` URL, but a specific range PBF 404s. | Ensure the `{fontstack}` folder names match the `text-font` strings, and that the server hosts all required ranges (0-255, 256-511, …). |
| Native app shows no labels at all | Native does not support omitted `glyphs`; you need a `glyphs` URL **or** a `font-faces` entry that points at a local file. | Add a `glyphs` URL, or bundle the TTF/OTF on the device and reference it via `font-faces`. |
| Labels are missing only for Arabic/Hebrew/Devanagari | Using an old GL JS version (< 6.7) that cannot shape complex scripts locally. | Upgrade to GL JS ≥ 6.7 (preferably latest) or load the RTL plugin for pre-6.9 versions. |
| CJK characters look blocky or are missing | `localIdeographFontFamily` set to `false` (or you forced glyph PBFs for CJK). | Remove that setting or set it back to the default (`'sans-serif'`). CJK is best rendered locally via TinySDF. |
| Font loads in Chrome but not in Firefox | CORS header missing on the font file. | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) to the font response. |
| Font loads locally but the style still shows the generic font | `text-font` list does not include the exact family name you loaded. | Ensure the first entry in `text-font` matches the `font-family` you defined. Example: `"text-font": ["Noto Sans", "sans-serif"]`. |

**How to verify which path is being used**

```js
map.on('styledata', () => {
  console.log('glyphs URL:', map.getStyle().glyphs);
  console.log('font-faces:', map.getStyle()['font-faces']);
});
```

- If `glyphs` is `undefined` → you’re in local-font mode.  
- Open the browser dev-tools → **Network** → filter “font”. You should see the TTF/OTF request (if you used `font-faces`) or nothing (if you rely on system fonts).  

---

## 5. TL;DR checklist for your situation

1. **You have no `glyphs` field** → MapLibre is using the *local-font fallback*.  
2. **Your `text-font` name isn’t matching any loaded font** → the browser falls back to a generic family → you see the wrong font.  
3. **Fixes**  
   - Load the desired font via a normal CSS `@font-face` (or a Google-Fonts `<link>`).  
   - Or, add a `font-faces` entry in the style that points at the TTF/OTF and make sure the name matches.  
   - If you need identical rendering on Native, either keep a `glyphs` URL **or** use `font-faces` (Native reads it from version 11.13.0 / 6.18.0 onward).  
4. **No bug** – this is the intended behavior introduced in GL JS v5.11.0.  

---

### Quick code example (GL JS only)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – local font example</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    @font-face {
      font-family: "MyBrand";
      src: url("https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
    }
    body { margin:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map" style="width:100vw;height:100vh;"></div>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "sources": {
        "osm": { "type": "raster", "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"], "tileSize": 256 }
      },
      // No glyphs property!
      "layers": [
        {
          "id": "background",
          "type": "background",
          "paint": { "background-color": "#f8f4f0" }
        },
        {
          "id": "roads",
          "type": "raster",
          "source": "osm"
        },
        {
          "id": "city-labels",
          "type": "symbol",
          "source": "osm",
          "layout": {
            "text-field": ["get", "name"],
            "text-font": ["MyBrand", "sans-serif"],
            "text-size": 14
          },
          "paint": {
            "text-color": "#222"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, inspect the **Network** tab → you’ll see **no** `*.pbf` requests, only the font file being fetched. The labels will be rendered with *MyBrand*.

---

## 6. When you *do* want a glyph server again

If you later decide that you need the classic SDF glyph workflow (e.g., you want the exact same rasterised shapes on every device, or you need to support a brand-specific font on Native without bundling the whole TTF), just add a `glyphs` URL back to the style and make sure the folder names match the `text-font` strings. The local-font fallback will be bypassed, and MapLibre will request the appropriate `{fontstack}/{range}.pbf` files.

